./docs/dev/structured-surface-architecture-2026-06-27.md:439:### LENS REFRAME — lenses as named views over the Surface (selection + render-only encoder), authored low-ceremony, with a per-vertex view vocabulary that resolves open-Q #4
./docs/dev/structured-surface-architecture-2026-06-27.md:440:A lens stops being a from-scratch (FoldState)->Block emitter and becomes a NAMED VIEW = (an ordered composition of the keystone's Surface->Surface transforms) + (an optional render-only encoder). Three ceremony tiers: (A) declared-as-data in a new per-vertex `views {}` KDL block — zero Python, compiles to filter/search/select/budget/order over the Surface; (B) declared selection + a thin `fold_view(surface,zoom,width)->Block` encoder for a novel render shape; (C) a pure `transform(surface)->surface` module for derived projections the fixed transforms can't express (reconcile's cross-section attention bucket). The 4-tier file resolution is untouched for B/C; declared views resolve from the vertex itself. The lens-vs-flag boundary becomes crisp: CLI flags ARE the Surface algebra applied inline; a view is a saved, named expression in that same algebra. Open-Q #4 resolves AS-GROUNDED: vertices ALREADY declare their own kinds/folds/search/preview (the `loops {}` block keys ARE the kinds; there is no enforced global table — the CLAUDE.md kind table is a shared STARTER convention, not a schema). `views {}` adds the missing third leg, making the long tail (self/intention/incident/practice) first-class by construction: a vertex declares the kind AND a named view over it, both as data, no global-registry edit, no Python.
./docs/dev/structured-surface-architecture-2026-06-27.md:442:REFRAME THESIS. The keystone fixes the lens to render-only over the Surface (fold_view(surface,zoom,width)->Block, no salience/grouping/budget math). This facet designs the AUTHORING surface on top of that. A lens NAME resolves to a VIEW = (selection over the Surface) + (an encoder). Selection is a composition of the keystone's already-defined transforms (search/filter/select/budget/count/whole). The encoder is the keystone's render() (default) or a per-view render-only fold_view. Crucially, EVERYTHING a lens does today splits cleanly into these two halves — verified by reading the real long-tail lenses: session_start.py (500 lines) and reconcile.py (317 lines) are ~80% selection+salience+grouping+budget that the Surface now materializes, and ~20% genuine render. session_start.py literally copy-pastes _compute_inbound_refs/_salience/_inbound_count out of fold.py; 3 of the 7 user-global lenses re-implement salience. The reframe DELETES that 80%.\n\nTHE THREE CEREMONY TIERS (the ergonomics Kyle cares about):\n\nTIER A — DECLARED VIEW (zero Python, the new low-ceremony default). A new `views {}` block in the .vertex file declares a named view as pure selection. It compiles to a fixed Surface->Surface pipeline: filter(kind,key,where,observer) -> search(match) -> select(fields) -> budget(per-kind/last/limit) with an order/granularity hint, then the DEFAULT render() encoder. KDL shape, validated at parse time:\n  views {\n    open-work { kinds \"thread\" \"task\"; where \"status\" \"!=\" \"resolved\"; order \"recency\" }\n    landing   { kinds \"task\" \"thread\" \"session\" \"decision\"; per-kind \"task\" 10 \"thread\" 15; fields \"status\" \"message\" }\n  }\nThe declared keys map 1:1 onto keystone transforms: `kinds`->filter(kind in ...) AND custom kind-order (overrides the Surface default of vertex-declaration order); `where field op value`->filter(where=) using the keystone's fixed predicate (eq/neq/in/prefix), NOT a DSL; `key`->filter(key prefix); `match`->search(); `fields`->select(); `per-kind`/`last`/`limit`->budget(); `order` in {salience,ts,recency}; `granularity` in {headline,whole}. This is the missing middle between retyping a flag-pipeline and writing a 300-line module.\n\nTIER B — DECLARED SELECTION + RENDER ENCODER (small Python, only when the render shape is novel). The view declares selection as data AND points at a render module: views { landing { kinds ...; render \"landing\" } }. landing.py exports fold_view(surface,zoom,width)->Block that ONLY draws the tier headers / sublabels — it imports NOTHING salience- or fold-related because Row.salience/Row.inbound are already on the Surface. session_start's 500 lines collapse to a ~40-line encoder + a 4-line declaration.\n\nTIER C — TRANSFORM MODULE (the escape hatch, only for derived projections). When selection needs a derived field the fixed transforms can't compute — reconcile's three-bucket attention classification, which depends on cross-section session_ts + status-set membership + recency — the view points at a module exporting transform(surface)->surface (mirroring the existing lens fetch(vertex_path,**kwargs) convention). The transform stamps a `bucket` field onto each Row (reading the session rows already present on the Surface; it does NOT re-query), and the default render groups by it. This is a Surface->Surface function — the keystone's own contract — NOT a fold reimplementation. Constrained: pure, no store access, no re-fetch, no painted import. reconcile's 317 lines collapse to a ~30-line transform + the declaration.\n\nRESOLUTION (rides on the existing 4-tier chain, one small extension). Today _resolve_render_fn (cli/lens.py:72) is a 3-tier render-fn resolver and _find_lens_module_path (lens_resolver.py:181) does the 4-tier FILE search (vertex-local -> cwd -> user-global -> built-in). The extension: when `--lens NAME` (or a vertex view reference) is requested, FIRST consult the vertex's `views {}` declarations (parsed from VertexFile, sibling of the existing `lens` field); a declared view yields (compiled transform-pipeline, render module-or-default). Only if NAME is not a declared view does it fall through to the existing 4-tier file search for a fold_view module. `--lens` stays the flag (it is the measured, established one; no new --view flag — dead-surface discipline). transform modules resolve via a resolve_lens_transform mirror of the existing resolve_lens_fetch (lens_resolver.py:86), reusing _find_lens_module_path verbatim.\n\nLENS-VS-FLAG, RESOLVED SHARPLY. The keystone exposes the transform algebra as inline CLI flags (--match/--kind/--key/--where/--fields/--limit/--last/--count/--whole). A named view is a SAVED expression in that identical algebra. Decision rule: (1) use a FLAG for a one-off projection at the call site; (2) promote to a Tier-A DECLARED VIEW when the same flag-pipeline recurs — name it once, type `--lens NAME`, same params just saved; (3) escalate to Tier B/C only when selection needs a derived projection the fixed transforms can't express, or a non-default render shape. Because a declared view is selection applied BEFORE both encoders, `--lens NAME --json` and `--lens NAME` (plain) carry IDENTICAL windowed/projected rows — which fixes today's incoherence where --json bypasses the lens entirely (views/fold.py:312) and silently drops the view's selection.\n\nOPEN-Q #4 (per-vertex vocabularies), RESOLVED AS-GROUNDED. The premise 'one global table vs long-tail-as-drift' is already settled by the code in loops' favor: VertexFile.loops is dict[kind -> LoopDef] (loader.py:745-751) — each `loops {}` child node name IS a kind the vertex declares, and each LoopDef carries its own fold (FoldDecl), search fields (FTS index), and preview fields. KindView in the keystone is built FROM exactly these. There is NO enforced global kind registry anywhere; the CLAUDE.md 'Kind selection by intent' table is a shared STARTER CONVENTION (a default vocabulary you inherit), not a closed schema. So long-tail kinds are NOT drift — they are first-class the instant a vertex declares them. What was MISSING is the third leg: per-vertex declared VIEWS. Today `lens {}` (loader.py:597) only declares two Python module pointers (fold/stream). `views {}` completes the vocabulary: kinds+folds+search+preview (existing) + named views (new), all per-vertex, all as data. DISSOLUTION NOTE: `lens { fold \"X\" }` is the degenerate view (whole-surface selection + custom render), so `lens {}` dissolves into `views {}` — only 2 vertices (identity, comms) use it; the clean end-state rewrites those 2 and the loader keeps `lens {}` as deprecated sugar that compiles to ViewDecls, sweeping the residue rather than carrying two mechanisms.
./docs/dev/structured-surface-architecture-2026-06-27.md:445:- **net-new:** ViewDecl frozen dataclass in libs/lang/ast.py (kinds, key, where-predicate, match, fields, per-kind/last/limit budget, order, granularity, optional render/transform module names) + `views {}` parser in loader.py (sibling of _load_lens_block) + validator: kebab-case names, where-predicate shape, and an orphan-kind guard (declared view `kinds` must be a subset of the vertex's `loops {}` kinds — reuses the per-vertex vocabulary, fails loud on a typo'd kind); The view COMPILER: ViewDecl -> (Surface -> Surface) that binds declared params onto the keystone transforms. ~one small function in apps/loops; the only genuinely new logic, and it is a thin adapter over transforms the keystone already defines; Resolution wiring: extend _resolve_render_fn / _effective_lens_name (cli/lens.py) so `--lens NAME` consults the vertex `views {}` block BEFORE the 4-tier file search; add resolve_lens_transform (lens_resolver.py) mirroring resolve_lens_fetch; Migration of the 7 user-global lenses + built-in fold lens from fold_view(FoldState) to fold_view(Surface) / the transform+encoder split. The keystone already OWNS the FoldState->Surface seam decision; this is the residue of it. Each lens shrinks. call_lens's TypeError fallback (lens_resolver.py:237) must be replaced with signature inspection so a half-migrated lens's TypeError isn't silently misread as a signature mismatch (keystone gotcha); Dissolve `lens {}` into `views {}`: rewrite the 2 vertices (identity, comms) that use it, keep the loader accepting `lens {}` as deprecated sugar that compiles to ViewDecls, then sweep the residue (LensDecl, _load_lens_block, the fold/stream fields on VertexFile); Docs sweep: lenses/CLAUDE.md contract -> render(surface,...) + the `views {}` authoring guide and the three ceremony tiers; the root CLAUDE.md 'Kind selection by intent' table reframed from 'the kinds' to 'the default starter vocabulary — vertices declare their own kinds AND views'; golden snapshot tests regenerated in the same sweep
